@page "/kretzmann"
@using BibleAtlas.Client.Contracts
@using BibleAtlas.Client.Explore
@using BibleAtlas.Client.State
@using BibleAtlas.Client.Views
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject StateAtom<Locus> LocusAtom
@inject StateAtom<ViewArrangement> ViewArrangementAtom
@inject AtlasClient Atlas
@inject IExplorableClient Graph
@inject ViewRegistry Registry
@inject IJSRuntime JS

@* Batch CORPREAD-2 (ticket K2, owner verdict, VERBATIM, the batch's own
   charter: "kretzmann - wrong. Essentially this should be a copy of the
   main reader, except the kretzmann commentary is interleaved with the
   Bible. verses and partial verses are still clickable and you should get
   the same exploration results per node. container names should be the
   same. bible bolded. red letters should be there. layout of the page the
   same as reader.") -- REBUILDS CORPREAD-1b's own commentary-first page
   (a plain list of commentary items grouped by a quiet verse-anchor label,
   NO Bible text rendered at all -- see git history, batch-corpread1b-
   report.md §3) into THE READER ITSELF, with Kretzmann's own commentary
   interleaved after each verse.

   THE SHARED-CONTAINER LAW (deliverable 0a, CONTRACT.md): every scripture
   row below renders through the SAME reader components Reader.razor's own
   chapter body uses -- VerseLine.razor (verse number, verse text, xref
   marker), which itself renders MentionText.razor -> MentionScan.razor for
   place/person mentions and the words-of-christ red-letter wrap. NOT a
   copy, NOT a parallel mini-implementation -- the identical component
   instances, called from this page's own @foreach the same way Reader.razor
   calls them, with the SAME CSS classes (verse-line/verse-num/verse-text/
   verse-mention/verse-xref-marker/words-of-christ) and, whenever this page
   is NOT split (no simultaneous second reader pane on screen to collide
   with), the SAME testid values too ("verse-line-{n}", "verse-num-{n}",
   "verse-mention-{n}-{placeId}", "verse-xref-marker-{n}") -- a conformance
   Playwright test (kretzmann.spec.ts, KRETZMANN-2S) pins that these
   selectors match on BOTH /read and /kretzmann. THE ONE DELIBERATE
   TYPOGRAPHIC DELTA (declared in CONTRACT.md, per the brief): scripture
   text renders BOLD here (an added CSS class layered onto VerseLine's own
   TextClass parameter, "verse-text kretzmann-scripture-text" -- a pure
   additive class-list widening, no component change) -- Kretzmann's own
   commentary is what the page is FOR, and bolding the scripture it
   comments on keeps the two registers visually distinguishable at a
   glance, per the owner's own explicit instruction.

   SPLIT-MODE TESTID DISAMBIGUATION (VerseLine.razor's own new TestIdSuffix
   wiring on VerseNumTestId, the ONE reader-component parameter addition
   this batch makes -- see that file's own header): Kretzmann's own split
   partner is Reader itself (ViewRegistrySetup.cs's own
   EnterSplitKretzmannHostsReader, unchanged from CORPREAD-1b) -- while
   split and following, BOTH panes render the SAME chapter through the SAME
   VerseLine component, which would otherwise emit a LITERALLY duplicate
   "verse-num-5"/"verse-line-5" testid across the two simultaneous
   instances. VerseTestIdSuffix below is "" (byte-identical to /read)
   whenever NOT split -- the conformance pin above always runs standalone,
   so it always sees the unsuffixed vocabulary -- and "-kv" only while
   split, the SAME "same vocabulary, disambiguated instance" discipline
   MiniReaderExpand.razor's own TestIdSuffix already establishes for VerseLine
   reuse inside a popover (that file's own header comment, verbatim
   precedent), applied here for the first time to two simultaneous TOP-LEVEL
   readers instead of a reader nested inside a popover.

   RED LETTERS, MENTIONS, XREF MARKERS: all free, by construction -- VerseLine
   renders them unconditionally from the SAME VerseOut/WordsOfChrist/XrefCount
   data Reader.razor already fetches (Atlas.Chapter, unchanged endpoint), so
   nothing about this page's OWN code renders a red span, a mention span, or
   an xref marker -- it only supplies the data and callbacks VerseLine
   already knows how to consume.

   SAME EXPLORATION RESULTS PER NODE: a verse-line click here pushes the
   SAME `new VerseNode(vref)` OnLineExplore/Reader.razor pushes for the
   identical vref -- ExplorerPopover then fetches and renders the SAME
   VerseDetail regardless of which page opened it (KRETZMANN-2S proves this
   directly: click GEN.1.1 on /read, click GEN.1.1 on /kretzmann, same
   popover title/body). Mention clicks (MentionText -> MentionScan) push the
   SAME PlaceNode/PersonNode. The in-prose citation scanner
   (Explore/ScriptureRefScan.cs via Components/ScriptureRefText.razor,
   AFFIRMED-KEEP, owner verdict 3 verbatim -- unchanged, carried over whole
   from CORPREAD-1b) pushes the SAME VerseNode a plain click would.

   PASSAGE-RANGE SHIFT-CLICK ("verses AND PARTIAL VERSES are still
   clickable"): Reader.razor's own anchor+extend passage-selection mechanic
   (READ-5/Task 15 -- a plain verse-num click sets an anchor; a shift-click
   against a different verse-num forms a range and shows a `.passage-chip`
   that opens a PassageNode) is MIRRORED here (OnVerseNumClick/SetAnchor/
   PassageRef/OpenPassage below, same shape, same fields) -- this is page-
   LOCAL interaction state in Reader.razor too (never componentized there),
   so mirroring it here is the SAME pattern Reader itself uses, not a second
   copy of a shared component. DISCLOSED, NARROWER SCOPE: Reader.razor's own
   Shift-held backdrop-inertness JS mechanism (reader.js's watchShiftRelease,
   the `reader-shift-active` class that makes the popover backdrop
   pointer-events:none while Shift is held, so a SECOND shift-click can land
   on a verse-num underneath an already-open popover) is NOT replicated here
   -- a real, narrow, accepted gap: if a popover happens to already be open
   when a shift-click sequence starts, the backdrop may intercept the second
   click. Kretzmann's own commentary-click surface (a much larger fraction
   of this page's own body than Reader's verse rows) makes this an even
   rarer collision than on /read; not built this round, flagged here rather
   than silently omitted.

   COMMENTARY INTERLEAVED (kretzmann.org model, per the owner's own words):
   each verse's own commentary item(s) render immediately AFTER that
   verse's VerseLine, inside the SAME `.kretzmann-verse-block` section --
   visually distinct (regular-weight parchment prose, `.kretzmann-item`,
   unchanged from CORPREAD-1b) from the bolded scripture immediately above
   it. Section-heading de-duplication (`IsSectionStart`, "renders WITH the
   prose, ALWAYS, whether that item's own text has arrived yet or not" --
   CORPREAD-1b fix round S-1/S-C1) and the AFFIRMED-KEEP scanner both carry
   over UNCHANGED. CORPREAD-1b's own separate `.kretzmann-verse-anchor`
   quiet marginal-mark button (a bespoke small label opening a VerseNode) is
   RETIRED WHOLE -- it is now strictly redundant with the real verse-num
   button and verse-line click VerseLine itself already provides; keeping
   both would be a second, competing way to explore the SAME verse, exactly
   the "parallel path" this batch's own reuse mandate forbids.

   LAZY-PROSE MACHINERY (lazyProse.js + SemaphoreSlim(8) + KRETZMANN-13
   guard + every-render rebind, CORPREAD-1b fix rounds 1/2): CARRIED OVER
   UNCHANGED -- `OnAfterRenderAsync`, `OnItemNearViewport`, `_proseFetchGate`,
   `_proseRequested`, `data-kretzmann-item-id`/`data-loaded` are all
   byte-identical to CORPREAD-1b's own shipped, twice-fixed mechanism; see
   each member's own comment below for the (unchanged) full incident
   history.

   FOLLOW/RELEASE (deliverable 0a/0b, THE FOLLOW-RELEASE LAW): unchanged
   from CORPREAD-1b -- `Following`, `_releasedBook`/`_releasedChapter`,
   `ToggleFollowClick`, the follow chip markup, all carried over verbatim.

   WHAT DIED (per the brief, verbatim): CORPREAD-1b's own commentary-first
   page structure -- a separate reading column that led with commentary and
   never rendered the Bible text at all. WHAT SURVIVES: the scanner, lazy
   loading, follow/release, the conformance declarations -- re-seated into
   this new, reader-shaped structure, per the brief's own words. *@

<PageTitle>Bible Explorer &mdash; Kretzmann</PageTitle>

<CompositionSplit HostName="@ViewNames.Kretzmann" SplitModeOverride="@SplitMode" HostPaneClass="split-pane-host"
                   OnArrangementChanged="OnArrangementChanged" @ref="_compositionSplit" Context="ctx">
    <div class="@($"kretzmann-page{ctx.HostPaneClassSuffix}")" data-testid="kretzmann-page">
        <article class="kretzmann-column reader-column">
            <div class="kretzmann-controls">
                <ScripturePicker OnApply="ApplyScriptureRef" ThemeClass="picker-parchment" CurrentBook="@_book" CurrentChapter="@_chapterNum" />

                @if (!ctx.IsSplitOpen)
                {
                    <button type="button" class="split-open split-open-parchment" data-testid="split-open-kretzmann"
                            @onclick="ctx.InvokeHatch">
                        Read beside the reader
                    </button>
                }
                else
                {
                    @* THE FOLLOW-RELEASE LAW: rendered ONLY while split
                       (mirrors World.razor's own follow-chip) -- unchanged
                       from CORPREAD-1b. *@
                    <button type="button" class="follow-chip follow-chip-parchment" data-testid="kretzmann-follow-chip"
                            aria-pressed="@(Following ? "true" : "false")" @onclick="ToggleFollowClick">
                        @(Following ? "Following the reader" : "Reading independently")
                    </button>
                }
            </div>

            @* Batch CORPREAD-2 (K2, "layout of the page the same as
               reader"): a real <h1>.chapter-head, the SAME markup Reader.razor
               itself renders (book/num spans, byte-identical typography) --
               CORPREAD-1b's own <h2 class="chapter-head kretzmann-chapter-head">
               modifier is retired along with the plain kretzmann-title/-intro
               strapline it used to sit under, which had no counterpart on
               /read at all ("layout of the page the same as reader" — the
               reader's own page opens directly on the chapter head, not a
               page-title/intro paragraph). data-testid stays
               "kretzmann-chapter-head" (unchanged, CONTRACT.md-pinned),
               distinct from Reader's own bare "chapter-head" testid (Reader's
               heading is ALSO independently explorable via ChapterNode/hover
               peek, a mechanism this batch does not add to Kretzmann -- a
               disclosed, narrower scope; a plain non-interactive heading
               here, same visual register). *@
            <h1 class="chapter-head" data-testid="kretzmann-chapter-head">
                <span class="chapter-head-book">@BookName</span>
                <span class="chapter-head-num">@_chapterNum</span>
            </h1>

            @if (_error is not null)
            {
                <div class="toast" data-testid="toast">
                    <p class="toast-message">@_error</p>
                </div>
            }
            else if (_loading)
            {
                <p class="kretzmann-loading">Loading&hellip;</p>
            }
            else
            {
                @* Batch CORPREAD-2 (K2): "kretzmann-empty" no longer gates
                   the WHOLE page -- CORPREAD-1b's own Kretzmann never
                   rendered scripture at all, so "no commentary for this
                   chapter" and "nothing to show" were the same condition.
                   Now the chapter's own real Bible text always renders
                   (the reader-parity mandate); a chapter this corpus
                   happens to carry NO commentary for simply shows the
                   scripture with no interleaved blocks, plus this one quiet
                   note -- never an empty page for a chapter that has real
                   verses. *@
                @if (!_rows.Any(r => r.CommentaryItems.Count > 0))
                {
                    <p class="kretzmann-empty" data-testid="kretzmann-empty">No commentary is indexed for this chapter.</p>
                }
                var xrefLetters = VerseLine.ComputeXrefLetters(_verses);
                @foreach (var row in _rows)
                {
                    @if (row.Verse.Heading is { } heading)
                    {
                        <PericopeHeading Heading="@heading" OnExplore="OnLineExplore" />
                    }
                    <section class="kretzmann-verse-block" data-testid="@($"kretzmann-verse-group-{row.Verse.Verse}")">
                        @* THE SHARED-CONTAINER LAW: the SAME VerseLine
                           component (and therefore the SAME testid/class
                           vocabulary) Reader.razor's own chapter body renders
                           -- see this file's own header for the full "why"
                           behind every parameter below. *@
                        <VerseLine Verse="@row.Verse" Book="@_book" Chapter="@_chapterNum" OnExplore="OnLineExplore"
                                   RowClass="verse-line explorable" RowTestIdPrefix="verse-line" TestIdSuffix="@VerseTestIdSuffix"
                                   VerseNumClass="verse-num" ShowVerseNumButton="true" VerseNumTestIdPrefix="verse-num"
                                   OnVerseNumClick="@(e => OnVerseNumClick(e, row.Verse.Verse))"
                                   TextClass="verse-text kretzmann-scripture-text" MentionClass="verse-mention"
                                   XrefMarkerGlyph="@xrefLetters.GetValueOrDefault(row.Verse.Verse, "")"
                                   OnXrefClick="OpenVerseXrefEntry" />

                        @foreach (var item in row.CommentaryItems)
                        {
                            @* CORPREAD-1b fix round (S-1/S-C1, unchanged):
                               the section heading renders WITH the prose,
                               ALWAYS -- while loading and after -- never
                               discarded once text arrives. IsSectionStart is
                               computed once, structurally, in
                               LoadChapterAsync -- not a markup-time dedup. *@
                            @if (item.IsSectionStart)
                            {
                                <h3 class="kretzmann-section-heading" data-testid="@($"kretzmann-section-heading-{Slug(item.Id)}")">@item.Heading</h3>
                            }
                            @* data-kretzmann-item-id/data-loaded: the lazy-load
                               observer's own hook -- see this file's own
                               header and wwwroot/js/lazyProse.js, unchanged. *@
                            <div class="kretzmann-item explorable" data-testid="@($"kretzmann-item-{Slug(item.Id)}")"
                                 data-kretzmann-item-id="@item.Id" data-loaded="@(item.Text is not null ? "true" : "false")"
                                 tabindex="0" role="button"
                                 @onclick="@(() => OnExplore(item))" @onkeydown="@(e => OnItemKeyDown(e, item))">
                                @if (item.Text is { } text)
                                {
                                    <ScriptureRefText Text="@text" Toc="@(_toc ?? EmptyToc)" ClassPrefix="kretzmann-ref"
                                                       OwnerSlug="@Slug(item.Id)" OnExplore="OnExploreNode" />
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <span class="kretzmann-item-loading" aria-hidden="true">&hellip;</span>
                                }
                            </div>
                        }
                    </section>
                }
            }

            <nav class="kretzmann-nav" aria-label="Chapter navigation">
                @if (PrevTarget is { } prev)
                {
                    <button type="button" class="kretzmann-nav-button" data-testid="kretzmann-prev"
                            aria-label="@($"Previous chapter: {prev.Name} {prev.Chapter}")"
                            @onclick="@(() => OnChapterNavClick(prev.Book, prev.Chapter))">&lsaquo; @prev.Name @prev.Chapter</button>
                }
                @if (NextTarget is { } next)
                {
                    <button type="button" class="kretzmann-nav-button" data-testid="kretzmann-next"
                            aria-label="@($"Next chapter: {next.Name} {next.Chapter}")"
                            @onclick="@(() => OnChapterNavClick(next.Book, next.Chapter))">@next.Name @next.Chapter &rsaquo;</button>
                }
            </nav>

            @* Batch CORPREAD-2 (K2, "partial verses are still clickable"):
               Reader.razor's own anchor+extend passage-range chip, mirrored
               -- see this file's own header for the full "why". *@
            @if (_passageRange is { } range)
            {
                <button type="button" class="passage-chip" data-testid="@($"passage-chip{VerseTestIdSuffix}")" @onclick="OpenPassage">@PassageRef(range)</button>
            }
        </article>
    </div>
</CompositionSplit>

@if (_activeNode is not null)
{
    <ExplorerPopover @key="_activeNode" Root="_activeNode" OnClose="ClosePopover" />
}

@code {
    // Fix round (S-1): IsSectionStart is computed ONCE, structurally, when
    // the listing resolves (LoadChapterAsync below) -- true exactly when
    // this item's own Heading differs from the immediately preceding item's
    // in canonical order (mirrors Concord.RenderRows's own part/article
    // change-detection). `with { Text = ... }` (the lazy-load update path,
    // UpdateItemText below) preserves it untouched -- a later prose arrival
    // never affects section-heading placement.
    private sealed record CommentaryItemRow(string Id, string Heading, string? Text, bool IsSectionStart);

    // Batch CORPREAD-2 (K2): a merged row -- this verse's own VerseOut
    // (rendered through VerseLine) plus whatever commentary items cover it,
    // interleaved directly beneath -- replaces CORPREAD-1b's own
    // commentary-only CommentaryVerseGroup (which never carried a VerseOut
    // at all).
    private sealed record MergedVerseRow(VerseOut Verse, List<CommentaryItemRow> CommentaryItems);

    private static readonly List<BookTocEntry> EmptyToc = new();

    [Parameter] public bool? SplitMode { get; set; }

    private CompositionSplit? _compositionSplit;

    private Projection<Locus>? _locusBacking;
    private Projection<Locus> _locus => _locusBacking ??= new Projection<Locus>(LocusAtom);

    // Batch CORPREAD-1b (deliverable 0a/ticket K, THE FOLLOW-RELEASE LAW):
    // unchanged.
    private bool Following => !(_compositionSplit?.IsSplitOpen ?? false) || ViewArrangementAtom.Value.Follow;

    private string? _releasedBook;
    private int? _releasedChapter;

    private string _book => Following ? _locus.Value.Book : (_releasedBook ?? _locus.Value.Book);
    private int _chapterNum => Following ? _locus.Value.Chapter : (_releasedChapter ?? _locus.Value.Chapter);

    // Batch CORPREAD-2: VerseLine.razor's own TestIdSuffix parameter (this
    // file's own header has the full "why") -- "" (byte-identical to /read)
    // whenever this instance is NOT split (the conformance pin,
    // KRETZMANN-2S, always runs standalone); "-kv" only while split, when a
    // simultaneous guest Reader pane could otherwise render the SAME verse's
    // testids twice.
    private string VerseTestIdSuffix => (_compositionSplit?.IsSplitOpen ?? false) ? "-kv" : "";

    private List<BookTocEntry>? _toc;
    private List<VerseOut> _verses = new();
    private List<MergedVerseRow> _rows = new();
    private bool _loading = true;
    private string? _error;
    private int _requestId;
    private IExplorable? _activeNode;
    private IEscapeHatch? _followHatchBacking;

    private string? _loadedBook;
    private int? _loadedChapterNum;

    // Batch CORPREAD-2 (K2, "partial verses clickable" -- Reader.razor's own
    // anchor+extend passage-range mechanic, mirrored; see this file's own
    // header). _anchorVerse is the last PLAIN click; _passageRange is null
    // unless a shift-click just formed a real range.
    private int? _anchorVerse;
    private (int From, int To)? _passageRange;

    // Fix round (Q-2, CRITICAL, CORPREAD-1b): the lazy-prose mitigation's
    // own state -- unchanged, see this file's own header and
    // wwwroot/js/lazyProse.js.
    private IJSObjectReference? _lazyJs;
    private DotNetObjectReference<Kretzmann>? _selfRef;
    private readonly HashSet<string> _proseRequested = new(); // one fetch per item, ever, per chapter generation
    private readonly SemaphoreSlim _proseFetchGate = new(8); // bounded ACTUAL concurrency, independent of when a fetch is requested

    private string BookName => _toc?.FirstOrDefault(b => b.Code == _book)?.Name ?? _book;

    // Wire ids ("CommentaryItem:kretzmann/0.1.0") carry ':' and '/' -- not
    // legal in a bare CSS-identifier-ish testid; a stable, readable slug for
    // the testid only (never used to re-derive the id itself).
    private static string Slug(string wireId) => wireId.Replace(':', '-').Replace('/', '-').Replace('.', '-');

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // Batch CORP-1: the one legitimate direct LocusAtom.Changed
        // subscription this view needs -- see ConformanceTests.cs's own
        // EffectsOnlyViaRegistry allowlist entry for the full justification.
        LocusAtom.Changed += OnLocusChanged;
        _selfRef = DotNetObjectReference.Create(this);

        try
        {
            _toc = await Atlas.Books();
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Non-fatal -- BookName falls back to the bare code; the
            // chapter/commentary fetches below do not need the TOC at all
            // except to resolve the scripture-ref scanner's own book-name
            // alternation (ScriptureRefText simply renders no explorable
            // refs until the TOC resolves).
        }

        await LoadChapterAsync();
    }

    // Fix round 2 (re-review finding, IMPORTANT -- CompositionSplit.razor's
    // own RULING 4(a), THE VC-1 hazard class, unchanged): rebind
    // UNCONDITIONALLY, every render, guarded only by "is my JS module
    // loaded" (`_lazyJs is not null`) -- see this file's own header.
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender)
        {
            try
            {
                _lazyJs = await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", "./js/lazyProse.js");
            }
            catch (JSDisconnectedException)
            {
                return; // navigated away mid-import -- nothing left to bind
            }
        }

        if (_lazyJs is null)
        {
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            await _lazyJs.InvokeVoidAsync("observeProseItems", _selfRef, "[data-kretzmann-item-id]");
        }
        catch (JSDisconnectedException)
        {
            // Navigated away mid-call -- nothing left to observe.
        }
    }

    private void OnLocusChanged()
    {
        // THE FOLLOW-RELEASE LAW: while released, this view browses
        // independently -- a shared-atom move is deliberately IGNORED.
        if (!Following)
        {
            return;
        }

        _activeNode = null;
        _anchorVerse = null;
        _passageRange = null;
        _ = LoadChapterAsync();
    }

    // Batch CORPREAD-1b: re-renders for the follow-chip's own live label,
    // and reconverges the loaded chapter whenever a split-close (or any
    // other arrangement change) flips `Following` back to true onto a
    // DIFFERENT locus than what is currently loaded.
    private void OnArrangementChanged()
    {
        if (_loadedBook != _book || _loadedChapterNum != _chapterNum)
        {
            _releasedBook = null;
            _releasedChapter = null;
            _activeNode = null;
            _ = LoadChapterAsync();
        }
        else
        {
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    private async Task ToggleFollowClick()
    {
        var wasFollowing = Following;
        if (wasFollowing)
        {
            // RELEASE: seed the view-local position from the CURRENT shared
            // value.
            _releasedBook = _locus.Value.Book;
            _releasedChapter = _locus.Value.Chapter;
        }

        _followHatchBacking ??= Registry.Get(ViewNames.Kretzmann).EscapeHatches.Single(h => h.Kind == HatchKinds.ToggleFollow);
        await _followHatchBacking.Invoke();

        if (wasFollowing)
        {
            StateHasChanged(); // released -- book/chapter identical at this instant, only the chip's own label changes
        }
        else
        {
            // RE-FOLLOW: reconverge -- drops the seed and reloads from
            // whatever the shared atom holds NOW.
            _releasedBook = null;
            _releasedChapter = null;
            _activeNode = null;
            await LoadChapterAsync();
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        LocusAtom.Changed -= OnLocusChanged;

        if (_lazyJs is not null)
        {
            try
            {
                await _lazyJs.InvokeVoidAsync("unobserveProseItems");
                await _lazyJs.DisposeAsync();
            }
            catch (JSDisconnectedException)
            {
                // The circuit is already gone during teardown -- nothing left to clean up JS-side.
            }
        }

        _selfRef?.Dispose();
    }

    // ScripturePicker's own composed sref -- Kretzmann only cares about
    // book+chapter (browses by chapter, matching Reader's own scope for
    // this control).
    private void ApplyScriptureRef(string sref)
    {
        var parts = sref.Split('.');
        if (parts.Length == 0 || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(parts[0]))
        {
            return;
        }

        var book = parts[0];
        var chapter = parts.Length > 1 && int.TryParse(parts[1], out var c) ? c : 1;
        NavigateTo(book, chapter);
    }

    private sealed record AdjacentChapter(string Book, string Name, int Chapter);

    private AdjacentChapter? PrevTarget => ComputeAdjacent(forward: false);
    private AdjacentChapter? NextTarget => ComputeAdjacent(forward: true);

    // Mirrors Reader.razor's own ComputeAdjacent exactly (crosses book
    // boundaries via the TOC; null -- hidden -- at GEN.1's prev and
    // REV.22's next). Unchanged from CORPREAD-1b.
    private AdjacentChapter? ComputeAdjacent(bool forward)
    {
        if (_toc is null)
        {
            return null;
        }

        var idx = _toc.FindIndex(b => b.Code == _book);
        if (idx < 0)
        {
            return null;
        }

        var entry = _toc[idx];

        if (forward)
        {
            if (_chapterNum < entry.Chapters.Count)
            {
                return new AdjacentChapter(_book, entry.Name, _chapterNum + 1);
            }
            return idx + 1 < _toc.Count ? new AdjacentChapter(_toc[idx + 1].Code, _toc[idx + 1].Name, 1) : null;
        }

        if (_chapterNum > 1)
        {
            return new AdjacentChapter(_book, entry.Name, _chapterNum - 1);
        }
        if (idx == 0)
        {
            return null;
        }

        var prevEntry = _toc[idx - 1];
        return new AdjacentChapter(prevEntry.Code, prevEntry.Name, prevEntry.Chapters.Count);
    }

    private void OnChapterNavClick(string book, int chapterNum) => NavigateTo(book, chapterNum);

    // THE FOLLOW-RELEASE LAW: the ONE choke point every navigation gesture
    // (picker, prev/next, passage-range clear) funnels through.
    private void NavigateTo(string book, int chapterNum)
    {
        _anchorVerse = null;
        _passageRange = null;

        if (Following)
        {
            LocusAtom.Dispatch(new SetLocus(book, chapterNum));
        }
        else
        {
            _releasedBook = book;
            _releasedChapter = chapterNum;
            _activeNode = null;
            _ = LoadChapterAsync();
        }
    }

    // Batch CORPREAD-2 (K2): fetches the chapter's own real VerseOut list
    // (Atlas.Chapter, the SAME endpoint Reader.razor calls) ALONGSIDE the
    // commentary listing (Atlas.KretzmannChapter, unchanged) -- two
    // independent fetches, run CONCURRENTLY (Task.WhenAll, this app's own
    // house pattern -- "independent fetches never serialize," MainLayout.
    // Continue's own precedent), never serialized. Renamed from
    // CORPREAD-1b's own LoadCommentaryAsync -- this method now loads the
    // WHOLE merged reading surface, not commentary alone.
    private async Task LoadChapterAsync()
    {
        var myRequest = ++_requestId;
        var book = _book;
        var chapterNum = _chapterNum;
        _loading = true;
        _error = null;
        StateHasChanged();

        try
        {
            var chapterTask = Atlas.Chapter(book, chapterNum);
            var commentaryTask = Atlas.KretzmannChapter(book, chapterNum);
            await Task.WhenAll(chapterTask, commentaryTask);

            if (myRequest != _requestId)
            {
                return; // superseded by a newer navigation -- discard this stale response
            }

            var chapter = chapterTask.Result;
            var doc = commentaryTask.Result;

            // Fix round (S-1, CRITICAL, CORPREAD-1b, unchanged): IsSectionStart
            // computed HERE, structurally, once -- true exactly when this
            // item's own Heading differs from the immediately preceding
            // item's, in canonical order, across verse boundaries.
            string? lastHeading = null;
            var commentaryByVerse = new Dictionary<int, List<CommentaryItemRow>>();
            foreach (var v in doc.Verses)
            {
                var items = new List<CommentaryItemRow>();
                foreach (var item in v.Items)
                {
                    var heading = item.Heading ?? "Commentary";
                    var isSectionStart = heading != lastHeading;
                    lastHeading = heading;
                    items.Add(new CommentaryItemRow(item.Id, heading, null, isSectionStart));
                }
                commentaryByVerse[v.Verse] = items;
            }

            _verses = chapter.Verses;
            _rows = chapter.Verses
                .Select(v => new MergedVerseRow(v, commentaryByVerse.GetValueOrDefault(v.Verse, new List<CommentaryItemRow>())))
                .ToList();
            _loadedBook = book;
            _loadedChapterNum = chapterNum;
            _loading = false;
            _proseRequested.Clear(); // fresh chapter -- a fresh lazy-load generation
            StateHasChanged(); // scripture + headings visible immediately -- prose loads lazily below, never blocking the whole chapter
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            if (myRequest != _requestId)
            {
                return;
            }

            _error = "Couldn't load this chapter — check your connection and try again.";
            _loading = false;
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    // Fix round (Q-2, CRITICAL, CORPREAD-1b, unchanged): called from
    // wwwroot/js/lazyProse.js's own IntersectionObserver, once per item, the
    // instant that item's own row approaches the viewport.
    [JSInvokable]
    public async Task OnItemNearViewport(string itemId)
    {
        if (!_proseRequested.Add(itemId))
        {
            return; // already requested (or a duplicate observer callback) -- fetch at most once per item per chapter generation
        }

        var myRequest = _requestId; // pins the chapter generation this request belongs to
        await _proseFetchGate.WaitAsync();
        try
        {
            if (myRequest != _requestId)
            {
                return; // superseded by a newer chapter navigation -- discard
            }

            string? text;
            try
            {
                var card = await Graph.Card(itemId);
                text = card.Description;
            }
            catch (Exception)
            {
                text = null; // fail soft -- the heading still renders, matches the app's own lazy-fetch degradation policy
            }

            if (myRequest != _requestId)
            {
                return;
            }

            UpdateItemText(itemId, text);
            StateHasChanged();
        }
        finally
        {
            _proseFetchGate.Release();
        }
    }

    private void UpdateItemText(string itemId, string? text)
    {
        _rows = _rows
            .Select(row => row.CommentaryItems.Any(i => i.Id == itemId)
                ? row with { CommentaryItems = row.CommentaryItems.Select(i => i.Id == itemId ? i with { Text = text ?? i.Heading } : i).ToList() }
                : row)
            .ToList();
    }

    private void OnExplore(CommentaryItemRow item) => _activeNode = new CommentaryItemNode(item.Id, item.Heading);

    private void OnItemKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e, CommentaryItemRow item)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Enter")
        {
            OnExplore(item);
        }
    }

    // Batch CORPREAD-2 (K2): VerseLine's own row/mention click path -- the
    // SAME `new VerseNode(vref)`/PlaceNode/PersonNode push Reader.razor's
    // OnLineExplore already performs.
    private void OnLineExplore(IExplorable node) => _activeNode = node;

    // Batch CORPREAD-2 (K2, "partial verses clickable" -- mirrors
    // Reader.razor's own OnVerseNumClick/SetAnchor exactly; see this file's
    // own header for the disclosed narrower scope). A plain verse-num click
    // sets the anchor only (no popover); a shift-click against a DIFFERENT
    // already-anchored verse forms a range and shows the passage chip
    // instead.
    private void OnVerseNumClick(MouseEventArgs e, int verseNum)
    {
        if (e.ShiftKey && _anchorVerse is int anchor && anchor != verseNum)
        {
            _passageRange = (Math.Min(anchor, verseNum), Math.Max(anchor, verseNum));
            _activeNode = null; // suppress the popover -- the chip is the result of a shift-click, not a new popover
            return;
        }

        SetAnchor(verseNum);
    }

    private void SetAnchor(int verseNum)
    {
        _anchorVerse = verseNum;
        _passageRange = null;
    }

    private string PassageRef((int From, int To) range) => $"{_book}.{_chapterNum}.{range.From}-{range.To}";

    private void OpenPassage()
    {
        if (_passageRange is not { } range)
        {
            return;
        }

        var text = string.Join(" ", _verses
            .Where(v => v.Verse >= range.From && v.Verse <= range.To)
            .Select(v => v.Text));
        _activeNode = new PassageNode(PassageRef(range), text);
    }

    // Batch CORPREAD-2 (K2, "red letters... mentions/clicks... identical"):
    // VerseLine's own xref-marker click path. DISCLOSED, NARROWER SCOPE
    // (matches MiniReaderExpand.razor's own established precedent, that
    // file's own header comment): commits only (click/Enter) -- Reader.
    // razor's own hover-preview micro-behavior (a debounced auto-dismiss,
    // OpenVerseXrefEntryHover/ScheduleHoverClose, tuned across several M-D3
    // fix rounds specifically for a top-level popover's own pane-anchored
    // open/dismiss dance) is not replicated here, the SAME "hover stays
    // Reader-page-only" boundary VerseLine.razor's own header already draws
    // for the popover's mini-reader.
    private void OpenVerseXrefEntry(int verseNum)
    {
        var vref = $"{_book}.{_chapterNum}.{verseNum}";
        if (_activeNode is VerseNode { XrefEntryPoint: true } current && current.Title == vref)
        {
            return; // already open on this exact verse's xref entry -- no-op, matches Reader.razor's own idempotent-reopen guard
        }

        _activeNode = new VerseNode(vref, xrefEntryPoint: true);
    }

    private void OnExploreNode(IExplorable node) => _activeNode = node;

    private void ClosePopover() => _activeNode = null;
}
